<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Deliberately empty: the App Sandbox is OFF, and that is a design decision
  rather than an omission.

  A system monitor and cleaner cannot work inside the sandbox. It enumerates
  every running process, reads IORegistry nodes for disk and battery, walks the
  user's caches to measure and delete them, lists /Applications to uninstall
  from it, and spawns package managers through CommandRunner. Each of those is
  something the sandbox exists to prevent.

  The protection model is therefore not the sandbox but PathSafety: an
  allowlist of exact directories, seven checks before any deletion, and
  re-validation immediately before touching a path. See the README.

  The one entitlement is Apple Events, which the Uninstaller uses to ask
  Finder to trash an app owned by root. Finder shows the password prompt;
  this app never sees the password and never runs as root.

  Hardened runtime is on (App.xcconfig) so the route to a signed, notarized
  release stays open.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
